Hypothesis (gap_activation_relu2_pos_encoding_rope): Switching the baseline to squared-ReLU activations together with rotary position embeddings (activation=relu2, pos_encoding=rope) will lower validation cross-entropy at the fixed 2,000-step budget relative to the GELU + learned-position baseline.
Mechanism: Both changes are reported to speed optimization. Squared ReLU reduced training cost and improved perplexity in Primer-style models. RoPE gave faster convergence and lower loss under the same number of training steps, including in a char-based model. Under a short fixed step budget, faster convergence should show up as lower validation loss, and the two changes act on different components (MLP nonlinearity versus attention position handling), so their benefits may add up.
Predicted direction: lower_val_loss
Falsification: Count against the hypothesis if the paired per-seed delta in validation cross-entropy (combined change minus baseline) is not consistently negative beyond the baseline's measured seed-to-seed noise. That includes deltas indistinguishable from noise and deltas that are positive.

